&lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;

&lt;p style="margin: 0pt; font-size: 10pt; font-family: &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;, Times, serif;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Income Taxes&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;Midla is not subject to federal or state income taxes; however,&amp;#xa0;&amp;#xa0;the related tax impacts of its operations are reported on the tax returns of Midla's sole member. For ratemaking purposes, Midla is treated as if it were a corporation that would require comprehensive income tax procedures.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;In accordance with FERC requirements, the accompanying financial statements include a hypothetical provision for federal and state income taxes as if Midla were a taxable entity following the guidance outlined in the Accounting Standards Codification 740, Income Taxes.&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Transportation and Exchange Gas Imbalances&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;In the course of providing transportation services, natural gas pipelines may receive different quantities of gas from shippers than the quantities delivered on behalf of those shippers.&amp;#xa0;&amp;#xa0;These transactions result in transportation and exchange gas imbalance.&amp;#xa0;&amp;#xa0;Such imbalances are recorded as Regulatory Assets or Regulatory Liabilities in the accompanying balance sheet using the posted index prices of the applicable approved tariffs, which approximate market rates.&amp;#xa0;&amp;#xa0;These imbalances are required to be resolved within a specified period of time or when certain quantity thresholds are met.&amp;#xa0;&amp;#xa0;Resolution can be through imbalance trading with other shippers or by utilizing the cash out procedures specified in each tariff. Exchange imbalances are settled with other pipelines through the cashout procedures in their tarriffs and recorded to the statement of income as Operating Expenses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Allocation of Salary and Overhead Expense Policy&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;Effective January 1, 2021 BBT began applying the Modified Massachusetts Formula("MMF") method based on a proportionate percentage of revenue, payroll costs and plant assets, which management believes to be reasonable in the circumstances. Such allocation totaled $63,822 and $$17,399,286 in 2025 and 2024, respectively.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Revenue Recognition&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;Our interstate transportation results are determined primarily by the amount of capacity customers reserve as well as the actual volume of natural gas that flows through the transportation pipeline. Under transportation contracts, our customers are charged (i) a firm transportation fee, which is a fixed fee regardless of how much natural gas is transported, (ii) an annual charge adjustment unit fee, which is based on every unit of volume of natural gas delivered utilizing rates as determined by the FERC, (iii) an interruptible transportation fee if applicable, or (iv) a combination of the three.&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;Revenue is recognized over the service period specified in the contract as the services are rendered using a time-based (passage of time) or units-based (units of service transferred) method for measuring provision of the services. Progress towards satisfying our performance obligation is based on the firm or interruptible nature of the promised service and the terms and conditions of the contract.&amp;#xa0;&amp;#xa0;Payment is generally received from the customer in the month of service or the month following the service. Contracts with customers generally are a combination of month-to-month and multi-year agreements. Revenues related to these services provided but not yet billed are estimated each month. These estimates are generally based on contract data, regulatory information, and preliminary throughput and allocation measurements.&amp;#xa0;&amp;#xa0;Final bills for the current month are billed and collected in the following month.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;In 2014, the Financial Accounting Standards Board ("FASB") issued Accounting Standards Update ("ASU") No. 2014-09, "Revenue from Contracts with Customers (Topic 606)", which supersedes most of the existing revenue recognition requirements in GAAP and requires entities to recognize revenue at an amount that reflects the consideration to which an entity expects to be entitled in exchange for transferring goods or services to a customer. It also requires significantly expanded disclosures regarding the qualitative and quantitative information of an entity's nature, amount, timing and uncertainty of revenue and cash flows arising from contracts with customers.&amp;#xa0;&amp;#xa0;We are not adopting the comprehensive disclosure requirements of ASC 606, as the Company is not required to do so for FERC reporting purposes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Use of Estimates&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;The preparation of financial statements in conformity with FERC regulations requires management to make estimates and assumptions that affect the reported amounts of certain assets and liabilities at the date of the financial statements and the reported amounts of expenses and/or revenues during the year.&amp;#xa0;&amp;#xa0;Actual results could differ from those estimates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Asset Retirement Obligations&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;Midla follows FERC Order No. 631, Accounting, Financial Reporting and Rate Filing Requirements for Asset Retirement Obligations ("ARO"), which addresses accounting and reporting for asset retirement obligations associated with the retirement of long-lived tangible assets.&amp;#xa0;&amp;#xa0;Order No. 631 requires entities to record the fair value of a liability for the retirement obligation in the period in which the liability is incurred, if a reasonable estimate of fair value can be made.&amp;#xa0;&amp;#xa0;The associated retirement cost is capitalized as part of the carrying value of the underlying asset and is subsequently depreciated over the underlying asset's useful life.&amp;#xa0;&amp;#xa0;After initial recognition, the ARO is revised to reflect the passage of time through accretion and for changes in the estimate amount or timing of cash flows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;Legal obligations exist for certain of Midla's pipeline transmission assets.&amp;#xa0;&amp;#xa0;Midla has not recognized the related ARO's as of December 31, 2025, or 2024 as the ultimate retirement dates of the underlying assets are uncertain and the related retirement costs are not estimable.&amp;#xa0;&amp;#xa0;An ARO will be recognized in the period in which sufficient information exists to reasonably estimate the settlement dates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;Legal obligations exist for certain of Midla's right-of-way agreements due to requirements or landowner options to compel Midla to remove the pipe at the final abandonment.&amp;#xa0;&amp;#xa0;Midla has not recognized an ARO as of December 31, 2025 and 2024.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family: Times New Roman; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0pt 0pt 0pt 18pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

